# ═══ NO `serves` LINE — surface nothing documented displays ═══
#
# All three are the SAME kind, and it is a kind habits did not produce: code
# built for something docs/DESIGN.md explicitly parked under "Explicitly deferred
# (roadmap, not v1)". That is not waste and it is not a bug — it is a bet on a
# roadmap item, taken early, in a repo whose own delivery plan has not reached
# the UI yet. Whether that bet is still wanted is his call, and the value here is
# that it is now a list rather than a feeling.
#
# BEH-LANG-1 has since been answered by his 10-12 23:00 note, and now
# describes what the code does; the other two are still open.
#
# ⚠️ Do NOT read this as "delete these". habits produced two unserved behaviours
# that needed opposite fixes; these three need a third answer again.

behaviour BEH-LANG-1 "The app drills one language, fixed in code, and every word still records its language"
  source defined language-vocab.notes.md#2026-10-12-2300
  priority 5
  review unreviewed
  contract PoolService.Language ("zh") is the one drilled language; no route or service method takes a language argument
  contract the Language column stays on every vocab row and every seed file, so a second language is a signature change, not a data migration
  # His middle ground ("one language for now ... is there a good middle ground?"),
  # and what the code has done since 9f49187 (10-13): the old threaded-parameter
  # version and its isolation test are gone.

behaviour BEH-ITEMTYPE-1 "A vocabulary item has a type, defaulting to word"
  source inferred backend/Balenthiran.LanguageVocab.Tests/SeedLoaderTests.cs:Load_defaults_item_type_to_word
  priority 4
  review unreviewed
  contract the schema reserves a Sentence type that nothing produces or consumes
  asks "The string `Sentence` occurs exactly twice in this repo: the enum member, and the DESIGN.md line reserving it. No seed entry sets a type, no route exposes one, the frontend has no such field. Does the reservation stay, or does the type arrive with sentence mode?"
  option "Keep the reservation" "VocabItemType.cs keeps `Sentence = 1`. It pins `Word = 0` as the default, so the enum can be extended later without renumbering a persisted column, and it makes DESIGN.md:34-35's second phase legible in the code a reader actually opens."
  option "Drop it until it is built" "The enum becomes `Word = 0` alone. Adding a member back later is one line and no migration, since nothing has ever persisted a 1. The type column, the nullable SeedEntry.Type and the defaulting test all survive — only the unreachable value goes."
  recommend "Keep the reservation" "This is the cheapest kind of reserved surface there is: an enum member costs no route, no client field, no migration and no test, and it is the one place a persisted integer genuinely benefits from being fixed early — `Word = 0` is only guaranteed if nothing renumbers around it. It is also already documented, which is the distinction this corpus is built to draw: reserved-and-written-down is not the same as surface nobody asked for."
  against "You have one other behaviour in this corpus (BEH-LANG-1) making the same bet on the same deferred roadmap, and answering both the same way is how a v1 quietly acquires a v2's shape. `Word = 0` is the C# default for any enum whether or not a second member exists, so the renumbering argument protects against a hazard that only appears once someone adds a value — at which point they can append it. And a reserved member is not free in the way it looks: it is a branch every future `switch` has to handle or deliberately ignore, on a code path nothing can currently reach."

behaviour BEH-DETERMINISM-1 "Selection is deterministic for a given seed"
  source inferred backend/Balenthiran.LanguageVocab.Tests/PoolServiceTests.cs:SelectNext_is_deterministic_for_the_same_seed
  priority 4
  review unreviewed
  contract the same seed picks the same word, so the sampler is testable
  asks "SelectNextAsync takes a required `int seed` and NOTHING calls it outside the tests — the drill API is PR 4 and does not exist. So where the seed comes from in production has never been decided, and it is a user-visible choice, not an implementation detail. Which is it: the client sends a seed, or the server makes one?"
  option "The server generates it per request" "The drill route makes a fresh seed each call. Determinism reverts to what DESIGN.md:66-67 says it is for — unit-testability — and every draw is independent. A refresh gives a different card. Resuming a session mid-way is then a separate feature with its own state, not something the seed gives you."
  option "The client supplies it per session" "The frontend mints a seed when a drill starts and sends it with each draw. The same session replays the same sequence, so a refresh or a reconnect resumes where it was rather than reshuffling. It also makes the sequence reproducible from a bug report. Cost: the seed is now part of the public API contract, and a client that reuses one gets the same word forever."
  recommend "The server generates it per request" "DESIGN.md:66-67 says the determinism exists so the sampler is unit-testable, and that is a claim about the test suite, not a promise to a user. A4 says the picker samples weakness-weighted from the active pool — that is a property of the WEIGHTS, and it holds either way, so the seed is buying nothing the design asks for. Keeping it server-side also keeps the failure mode small: a client that mishandles a seed can pin itself to one word, and that bug is invisible until someone notices they keep seeing 好."
  against "You are deciding this now, in a corpus, for a route that will be written in PR 4 — and the version that is harder to add later is the one being rejected. Making the seed part of the request from day one costs one optional query parameter and no state; retrofitting it means changing a shipped drill endpoint and regenerating the client. Resume-after-refresh on a phone is also not an exotic want for a drill app, and the pinned-to-one-word failure is preventable by advancing the seed per draw rather than by hiding it. There is a third answer this question does not offer: derive the seed from something stable you already have — user plus date — which gives a reproducible day without either a client contract or a decision about session state."

# ═══ His 2026-10-15 Kit notes: learning modes and vocab packs (kit#284) ═══
# "what we have now seems like a practice mode but i have no learning mode" and
# "i would love to add a mahjong vocab pack". Pending: specified, not built.

behaviour BEH-LEARN-1 "Learn mode shows five new words one at a time, hanzi, pinyin and English together"
  source defined language-vocab.notes.md#2026-10-15-2300
  priority 1
  actor learner
  when opens page:Learn
  then sees region:HanziLarge
  then sees region:PinyinUnderIt
  then sees region:EnglishMeaning
  when activates button:Next
  contract five words a round; nothing is asked until all five have been shown

behaviour BEH-LEARN-2 "After the five, the learner is quizzed on them: shown pinyin, recall the English"
  source defined language-vocab.notes.md#2026-10-15-2300
  priority 1
  actor learner
  when opens page:Learn
  then sees region:PinyinPrompt
  when fills field:Answer with ?english
  then sees region:Verdict
  contract only the five just shown are asked, so the round is learnable rather than a test of everything

behaviour BEH-LEARN-3 "The reverse quiz, English to pinyin, is a separate harder option"
  source defined language-vocab.notes.md#2026-10-15-2300
  priority 3
  actor learner
  when opens page:Learn
  when activates button:ReverseDirection
  then sees region:EnglishPrompt
  contract off by default; he called it "harder so maybe that is another option again"

behaviour BEH-PACK-1 "Words can be learned in themed packs, starting with a mahjong pack"
  source defined language-vocab.notes.md#2026-10-15-2308
  priority 2
  actor learner
  when opens page:Packs
  when activates button:MahjongPack
  then sees region:HanziLarge
  contract the mahjong pack holds the tile vocabulary: the numbers, the three suits, winds and dragons
  contract a pack is a seed file naming its words by hanzi, so a word can sit in a list and in any number of packs (language-vocab#19)
